          {
            "evidence_id": "runbook:connection-pools",
            "kind": "runbook",
            "content": {
              "title": "Investigate connection pool exhaustion",
              "body": "For a pool acquire timeout, compare checked-out connections, configured pool capacity, waiting requests, and observed returns. Pool exhaustion in one application does not imply that the database server has reached its connection limit.\n\nRead database session states and connection counts when available. Active work, idle transactions, and idle borrowed connections support different explanations. Compare resource ownership with completed requests before claiming a leak. A shared HTTP connection pool can be occupied by long-lived requests even when the upstream service answers independent probes.\n\nAlso check whether the process can create new sockets. A file-descriptor limit failure occurs before an upstream response and does not prove that the upstream rejected a connection. Report the observed resource limit and its scope. Increasing every client pool can worsen a server-wide connection shortage; do not recommend it without capacity evidence."
            }
          }
        ],
        "truncated": false,
